## Summary
Authored `lessons/amplituda-faza-i-speckle-ziarnistosc-ktora-nie-jest-szumem-gaussowskim.json` (module **m2**, 15 min) for the SAR course, following `generate_lesson/SKILL.md` end-to-end. It **passes `LessonSchema.safeParse`**.
**7 sections** (2 theory beats + 5 widgets, no back-to-back theory, ends on a widget):
1. **theory** — *Otwórz piksel: amplituda i faza* — complex SLC pixel, $I=A^2$ with full per-symbol gloss, phase intuition; inline phasor diagram; RGB bridge (audience override respected).
2. **quiz** — phase misconception ("just noise to throw away").
3. **theory** — *Speckle: koherentne nakładanie ech* — coherent phasor sum, multiplicative model $I=R\cdot n$, "don't reach for `cv2.GaussianBlur`", ENL $=\mu^2/\sigma^2$; inline random-walk-of-phasors diagram.
4. **plotImage** — std-vs-mean proving speckle's multiplicative signature (additive flat vs speckle rising); runnable `sourceCode`, axes/units/title/legend.
5. **quiz** (multi-select) — the headline misconception "speckle is/isn't Gaussian noise."
6. **parametricExplorer** — window-size slider on a single-look speckle sim: ENL rises (≈okno²) but the ship dot and edge smear — delivering the lesson summary's "uśrednianie sąsiadów nie działa jak w RGB."
7. **code** — `enl()` on the **genuine Sentinel-1 tile** (`/inputs/sar-echo-tile.png`), satisfying the course's "≥1 widget with real I/O on real tiles" mandate. All 4 tests verified green against the real tile.
**Assets generated on disk** (all referenced paths resolve, zero external URLs): two inline phasor diagrams, the additive-vs-multiplicative plot, plus reuse of the existing real tile. **5 lesson-level sources** (mix of paper/article/book) copied from `sources.md`, with section-level sources on theory beats.
One note: the ParametricExplorer ENL demo uses a clearly-framed *synthetic* single-look speckle simulation rather than the real tile, because the staged GRD tile is already heavily multilooked (flat-water ENL≈78, almost no residual speckle) and ParametricExplorer has no `/inputs/` mount — so a sim is the only honest way to show speckle reduction vs. looks. The real-tile requirement is met by the `code` widget.
