## Summary
Authored `courses/sar-.../lessons/cfar-krok-po-kroku-adaptacyjny-prog-ktory-wykrywa-statki-na-morzu.json` end-to-end per the `generate_lesson` skill.
**Lesson shape** (m4, 18 min, Polish — matching the course; 0.5 theory/practice → balanced, hands-on-leaning):
1. **theory** — why a single global threshold drowns in false alarms on rough sea (CFAR ≈ statistics-driven `adaptiveThreshold`); inline genuine-tile image
2. **plotImage** — global vs. adaptive threshold over a calm→rough sea profile (Rys. 1)
3. **theory** — the test/guard/background window + load-bearing formula $T = \mu + \alpha\sigma$, every symbol glossed with unit + typical value; inline window diagram
4. **parametricExplorer** (centrepiece) — Pfa + guard + background sliders on a synthetic sea-with-ships scene; live hits-vs-false-alarms readout (pure numpy, since the explorer auto-loads only numpy+matplotlib)
5. **quiz** — the Pfa trade-off (single-select; distractors from the "CFAR = global thresholding" misconception)
6. **code** — implement CA-CFAR on a **genuine Sentinel-1 sub-tile** mounted at `/inputs/` (cv2 box-sum ring stats), 4 deterministic synthetic tests + reference solution + live expected-output overlay
7. **theory** — pitfalls: target-masking → OS-CFAR, and land-clutter → mask the sea first
8. **sandbox** — free CFAR parameter play
3 theory beats (176/256/172 words) + 5 widgets; no adjacent theory; ends on a widget. 4 lesson-level sources (article/paper/book/paper) all from `sources.md`'s CFAR block.
