## Summary
Authored one lesson at `courses/sar-dla-inzyniera-computer-vision-od-piksela-echa-do-detektora-statkow-i-samolotow/lessons/transfer-learning-i-augmentacja-sar-klasyfikacja-celow-na-chipach.json` (module `m5`, 16 min), following `generate_lesson/SKILL.md` end-to-end.
**Structure** (6 sections, theory/practice ≈ 0.5): `theory → plotImage → quiz → theory → code → sandbox`
- **Theory 1** (250 w) — why naïve ImageNet transfer breaks on SAR: what transfers (shape/part layout in mid/high layers) vs. what breaks (low-level filters assume natural-image stats), with an RGB-vs-SAR contrast table and the practical recipe (dB normalize per-dataset, channel adaptation, freeze early layers, SAR augmentation). Inline optical-vs-SAR chip figure.
- **plotImage** — pixel-value histogram (optical vs SAR), full labelled/units axes + title + grid + legend; `sourceCode` reproduces the PNG byte-for-byte (verified).
- **Quiz** (multi-select) — diagnoses the misconception "CNNs can't learn on grayscale" against the three real reasons.
- **Theory 2** (230 w) — SAR-specific augmentation vs photometric jitter; the multiplicative speckle model `x̃ = x·s, s~Gamma(L,1/L), E[s]=1` with every symbol glossed (i, j, s, L, E[s]). Inline augmentation-grid figure.
- **Code** — implement `speckle_jitter`; 4 deterministic tests (1 visible smoke + 3 hidden), reference `solution` passes all four, mounted SAR chip input + live `outputMedia`.
- **Sandbox** — self-contained synthetic chip, free play with looks/rotations/flips.
**Decisions & checks**
- The schema's `SectionSchema` union has **no `image` type** (the SKILL's Image-widget guidance is stale here), so hero figures use inline markdown images + `plotImage` only.
- Real MSTAR/OpenSARShip chips aren't available via Copernicus (Sentinel-1 only), so I used **deterministic synthetic SAR-style chips** (gamma/multiplicative speckle), labeled as synthetic in alt text; the real datasets are cited in sources.
- `LessonSchema.safeParse` → **OK**; `npm run typecheck` clean; **no external image URLs**; all 5 referenced assets exist on disk; 5 lesson-level sources (4 curated papers + Wikipedia) plus section-level sources on both theory beats.
Nothing under `scripts/ralph/` was touched; no other lesson or `course.json` modified.
